00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:20.060
Great, now back to explaining Phenaki.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:25.910
Now, how hard is this problem of generating
long videos from text descriptions?

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:32.010
It’s quite hard for a lot of reasons,
here are a few: Videos are more than just

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.610
a sequence of images, they are a sequence
of coherent images.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.920
For example, it is hard to make the object
shown in the first frames look like in the

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:48.210
last frames, of course by accounting that
the viewpoint onto the object might have changed.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.340
Here you see an example where Phenaki struggles
with generating coherent video in terms of

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.180
the colour of the water, the exact details
of the teddy bear.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.950
Another problem is the availability of training
data.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.849
We have recently seen diffusion models generating
amazing images from text descriptions.

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:11.599
This is not an easy problem either, but there
are lots of available training data for this,

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:15.500
just think of all images and their captions
on the internet.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.120
The best thing about image captions is that
they do describe what we see in the images.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.790
This is very much unlike most of the text
associated with video.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:30.099
The most frequent type of text attached next
to videos are subtitles of what is being spoken,

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:35.030
but it’s mostly not a text description of
the visual scene or of what is happening in

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.030
the video.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.030
Don’t confuse subtitles with audio descriptions
for the visually impaired.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:41.140
Meaning that it is harder to find datasets
of videos paired with text descriptions.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:46.360
It’s not impossible though, Phenaki’s
authors cite for example WebVid of around

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.910
10 million videos annotated with text.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:55.670
But if we are to find long videos annotated
with text, then we are out of luck and we

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.220
start complaining about the inexistence of
data.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:03.250
And imagine, here we have Google complaining
about not enough data!

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:08.480
Okay then, if we do not have long video data,
then we will train a model without it, or

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.220
this is the point of Phenaki’s authors.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:16.299
The idea is to train Phenaki on short videos
of around 1.4 seconds

00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:23.030
and have the model iteratively produce a next
short video from a new prompt, but as a continuation

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.669
of the last one.

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:31.419
By doing this many times, one can produce
minute long videos and even potentially infinitely

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:35.660
long videos if we were to continue this iteration
until infinity.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.830
And this, even if the model was trained on
only short videos.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.100
Okay, but how?

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.680
Let’s get into more detail.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.440
What is Phenaki if not a diffusion model?

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.710
Let’s think about it by quickly answering
the following question: What is the leading

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.210
architecture of today that has taken over
every modality?

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.419
Yes, you are right!

00:04:57.419 --> 00:04:58.419
Transformers!

00:04:58.419 --> 00:05:04.290
Then we’ll be not too surprised to find
out that Phenaki is a system composed of multiple

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:05.290
transformers.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.560
To produce videos, we start from the text.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.980
We are doing deep learning here, so every
modality should be represented as a vector,

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:19.420
the authors use a pre-trained T5X language
model to produce word vectors from the text.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:24.430
And this will be somehow combined with video
to train to produce video from text.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:28.330
But we cannot understand that yet,
without first seeing how to process video

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.080
in the first place.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.100
Phenaki’s video processing is composed of
two parts.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:40.110
For the video part, we first have a transformer
that knows something about video only.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:47.830
You can think of this first transformer, called
C-ViViT, as of a neural video encoder or as

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:52.410
Ms. Coffee Bean likes to say, a dimensionality
reducer on steroids.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:59.110
Its job is to take in a video, which is this
huge chunk of data composed of many frames

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:06.370
and translate it into smaller dimensional
video embeddings, in other words video tokens.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:13.870
The second transformer called MaskGIT takes
these video embeddings of C-ViViT

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:19.289
and with the text embeddings, it produces
continuations of the videos matching the text

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:20.960
description.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.830
This was Phenaki at a high level, now let’s
get into more detail.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.080
How does the C-ViViT work?

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:32.630
We remember, the goal of this part of Phenaki
is to reduce the dimensionality of the video

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:33.630
data.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:39.460
So it is an encoder-decoder model trained
on just video data (no text here) to compress

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:45.520
the video with the encoder into video embeddings
and decompress it back to video again with

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:46.520
the decoder.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:53.820
The idea here with C-ViViT is to have a general-purpose
video encoder that having seen lots of video

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.849
data and having learned about the visual world,
can encode any video into video tokens and

00:06:59.849 --> 00:07:01.370
decode these back into video.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:07.150
But we stress, the authors want this encoder-decoder
to work for any video, meaning for variable

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:11.389
length video, so they decide for an autoregressive
architecture.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:18.550
In other words, they take the ViViT architecture
from prior work and make it causal, so autoregressive,

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.810
reconstructing video from left to right.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:26.280
The encoder takes in a sequence of video frames,
so ordered images.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.349
It extracts patches from the first frame,
and video patches, so stacks of patches at

00:07:31.349 --> 00:07:35.690
the same position through time, from the rest
of the video.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:41.199
The encoder applies a linear transformation
of the patches to reduce the dimensionality

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:42.990
to d.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:49.690
Transformer layers along the spatial dimension
with all-to-all attention combine the information

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:55.140
of each patch with information from its neighbouring
patches in the same frame.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:01.170
So now every patch is contextualized into
its own frame.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:06.610
Then transformer layers along the temporal
dimension combine each patch at a certain

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:12.909
position with patches at the same positions
that come from previous frames.

00:08:12.909 --> 00:08:18.129
Keep in mind that we are not allowed here
to look into the future, this is why the attention

00:08:18.129 --> 00:08:23.960
is causal here, meaning that we only look
to past frames for each given patch at a certain

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:24.960
frame t.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.110
Why is this causal attention important?

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.289
Because it allows us to embed variable length
videos,

00:08:31.289 --> 00:08:34.820
including videos of only one frame, so images!

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:39.930
It just means that the temporal transformer
layers on the image do not do much, but it

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:47.880
also means that the authors can train C-ViViT
not only on videos, but also on images, extending

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:53.890
the amount of data immensely and therefore
teaching C-ViViT as much about the visual

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:56.690
world as possible.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:09:04.000
The C-ViViT decoder then is a, we cite “upside
down version of the encoder”, meaning that

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:09.649
all the encoder operations are applied in
the reverse direction in the decoder to map

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:12.180
back to the pixel space of the video.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:18.560
Okay, so now we have explained C-ViViT that
trained on lots of video and images has learned

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:23.870
something about the visual world and can map
video to lower dimensional vectors

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:27.290
that hopefully capture the semantics of the
video.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:31.800
Because we need these semantic video embeddings
to work with them in the actual text-to-image

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.370
generation transformer, the MaskGIT.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:40.570
MaskGIT is not a new architecture, it has
already been established in previous work.

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:46.920
For MaskGIT, it is useful to think about BERT
a bit, so about masked language modelling.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.740
But now it’s multimodal.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:56.240
It takes in paired text and video and trains
to reproduce the video as following: The model’s

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:02.350
input for the text are the embeddings produced
by the pretrained T5X language model.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:10.019
The video input are the video tokens as produced
by C-ViViT.

00:10:10.019 --> 00:10:23.370
During training, the authors mask a ratio
of the video tokens with [MASK] tokens and

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:27.700
teach the network to reproduce the masked
video tokens.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:29.230
Very much like BERT.

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:33.480
To produce video from text during inference,
the model takes in the text embeddings and

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:37.940
just [MASK] tokens instead of video tokens,
since no video is produced yet.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:42.040
In a first step, it unmasks all video tokens.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:47.920
The C-ViViT decoder can take the produced
video tokens and decode them back to frames.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.040
Are we done?

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:54.670
No, since each [MASK] token is predicted in
parallel, thus not depending on the predictions

00:10:54.670 --> 00:11:01.780
of the other [MASK] token, the output here
is much likely incoherent.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:08.170
So, the authors discard some of the predictions
and mask a large fraction of the predicted

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.690
[MASK] tokens and let the model predict them
again.

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:16.050
But since now some video tokens are already
predicted, the next predictions are informed

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:21.690
of the video tokens from the last iteration
(and of course the text), so now the frames

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.899
should be more coherent.

00:11:23.899 --> 00:11:29.670
And in about 12 to 48 steps of predicting
all video tokens and masking a fraction again,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:32.380
MaskGIT produced the end video.

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:37.330
This was how MaskGIT produced short videos
as the authors trained it on short videos

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:43.930
paired with text description, since there
is no data for longer form videos.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:50.060
To produce long videos, the authors apply
MaskGIT iteratively, by first taking in a

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:55.800
sentence, no video and letting MaskGIT produce
the video as described previously.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:01.130
To prolong the video, it takes in a new sentence
description and the last frames of the previously

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:05.779
produced video and predicts a continuation
of the video, so masked tokens.

00:12:05.779 --> 00:12:10.120
You see, how this procedure can go on and
on to produce longer

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:13.820
and longer video with just linear scaling
of compute.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:20.610
To produce a 3 times longer video, one has
to apply MaskGIT 3 times, which is a benevolent

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:26.589
scaling unlike what a transformer applied
on longer sequences would give us – quadratic

00:12:26.589 --> 00:12:29.750
scaling due to the attention layer.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.610
This was it from us about how Phenaki works.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:37.680
We will not get into a lot of details of the
evaluation of this model, since we this is

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:42.820
where the paper really looks unfinished, like
the authors were in a hurry to get it out

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:43.930
as soon as possible.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:49.310
And the hurry makes sense, Phenaki was published
on the same day with another video generating

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:55.750
model from Google, called Imagen Video (see
our previous videos for this) which is a diffusion

00:12:55.750 --> 00:13:00.670
model capable of producing short videos, which
are much like animated images.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:06.779
And Phenaki and Imagen Video came out really
soon after MetaAI published their Imagen Video

00:13:06.779 --> 00:13:10.070
competitor, called Make-A-Video.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:14.640
For the explanation of those two models and
about a timeline of diffusion models, we can

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:19.440
leave you to watch our previous video, which
is exactly about this.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.550
See you next time!

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:25.610
Okay, bye!

